{"questions":[{"question_id":"1l1p8kjrk","paperTitle":"JEE Advanced 2013 Paper 1 Offline","year":2013,"examDate":2013,"subject":"physics","chapter":"dual-nature-of-radiation","chapterGroup":"modern-physics","topic":"photoelectric-effect","type":"integer","marks":3,"negMarks":0,"permalink":"pthe-work-functions-of-silver-and-sodium-are-46-and-23-e-jee-advanced-physics-units-and-measurements-vslatooywip3ziyu","content":"<p>The work functions of silver and sodium are 4.6 and 2.3 eV, respectively. The ratio of the slope of the stopping potential versus frequency plot for silver to that of sodium is ___________.</p>","options":[],"correct_options":[],"explanation":"<p>We have,</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.798ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.268ex\" height=\"3.023ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -983.5 5422.6 1336.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D449\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D449\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1046.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(2102.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(220,485) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"210E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-210E\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(576,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D453\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D453\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(453.3,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D452\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D452\"></use></g><rect width=\"996.2\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3561,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(4561.2,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(220,485) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D719\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D719\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(266,-345) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"1D452\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D452\"></use></g><rect width=\"621.4\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n<p>Slope is h/e.</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1l1qqx4r9/6ca66528-95e1-42fc-b273-c4e4ca1bdba9/7cf49140-b767-11ec-bc60-b7dd19a7dbd4/file-1l1qqx4ra.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/1l1qqx4r9/6ca66528-95e1-42fc-b273-c4e4ca1bdba9/7cf49140-b767-11ec-bc60-b7dd19a7dbd4/file-1l1qqx4ra.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0px auto;max-height:40vh\" alt=\"JEE Advanced 2013 Paper 1 Offline Physics - Dual Nature of Radiation Question 19 English Explanation\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/1l1qqx4r9/6ca66528-95e1-42fc-b273-c4e4ca1bdba9/7cf49140-b767-11ec-bc60-b7dd19a7dbd4/file-1l1qqx4ra.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/1l1qqx4r9/6ca66528-95e1-42fc-b273-c4e4ca1bdba9/7cf49140-b767-11ec-bc60-b7dd19a7dbd4/file-1l1qqx4ra.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1l1qqx4r9/6ca66528-95e1-42fc-b273-c4e4ca1bdba9/7cf49140-b767-11ec-bc60-b7dd19a7dbd4/file-1l1qqx4ra.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/1l1qqx4r9/6ca66528-95e1-42fc-b273-c4e4ca1bdba9/7cf49140-b767-11ec-bc60-b7dd19a7dbd4/file-1l1qqx4ra.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/1l1qqx4r9/6ca66528-95e1-42fc-b273-c4e4ca1bdba9/7cf49140-b767-11ec-bc60-b7dd19a7dbd4/file-1l1qqx4ra.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/1l1qqx4r9/6ca66528-95e1-42fc-b273-c4e4ca1bdba9/7cf49140-b767-11ec-bc60-b7dd19a7dbd4/file-1l1qqx4ra.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>Slope is the same for both silver and sodium.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the ratio of the slope of the stopping potential versus frequency plot for silver to that of sodium is 1 : 1.</p>"}]}